  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14093685/assets/639d18a8595c082eef85cd22e38b1ae6353b54c259078bf0c15a389ce0ace21f.png)\n\nFind the parametric equations of the hyperbola shown above given that its asymptotes are $y=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5}x.$",
  "worked_solution": "The parametric equations of a horizontal hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{sec}\u2061t,\\,y=b\\operatorname{tan}\u2061t,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are positive constants.\n\nThe vertices of the hyperbola occur at $(\\pm a,0).$ From the graph, we have that $(5,0)$ is a vertex of the hyperbola, so $a=5.$\n\nThe asymptotes of a horizontal hyperbola are $y=\\pm \\frac{b}{a}x.$ In our case, the asymptotes are $y=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5}x,$ and so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{b}{a} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{b}{5} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{5} \\\\ b & =\\sqrt{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{sec}\u2061t,\\,y=\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{tan}\u2061t.\n$$",
